    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.1007/s11160-026-10050-7",
    "abstract": "Abstract Predicting the response of oceanic apex predators to climate change is a critical frontier in marine science, essential for global food security and biodiversity conservation. For highly migratory tuna and billfish, such efforts have been hindered by fragmented data and an inability to disentangle the complex environmental drivers shaping their vulnerable early life stages across entire ocean basins. A lack of clarity regarding early life history introduces substantial uncertainty into population assessments and undermines climate-ready management. We overcome this long-standing challenge by applying a geostatistical framework to the most extensive record of larval tuna and billfish ever assembled. Our analysis provides the first basin-wide reconstruction of historical (1960\u201385) spawning habitats for nine key species in the Pacific Ocean. We project that ongoing warming will drive divergent, species-specific outcomes, with four commercially important tuna species facing habitat contraction while others, including Pacific bluefin tuna, find thermal refuge. Critically, the model identifies sea-water pH, phosphate concentration, and sea-surface height as powerful modulators of larval abundance, revealing that ocean acidification and nutrient dynamics present a compounding threat. The resulting spatially explicit forecasts are crucial for designing resilient marine protected areas and implementing dynamic, ecosystem-based fisheries management.",
    "field": "Fisheries ecology",
    "problem": "Forecasting the impacts of ocean warming on tuna and billfish is hindered by methodologically and geographically fragmented studies, which yield only localized insights and prevent a cohesive, basin-wide understanding of how multiple environmental stressors affect larval habitats and recruitment.",
    "method": "The authors apply a parsimonious geostatistical species-distribution model to a comprehensive historical larval-survey dataset, using latent predictors to deconvolve the complex effects of sea surface temperature and other environmental drivers on the distributions of tuna and billfish larvae in the Pacific Ocean.",
    "task": "The task is to project the impacts of ocean warming on tuna and billfish larval distributions across the Pacific Ocean by applying a geostatistical model to a comprehensive historical larval-survey dataset.",
